This module covers the principles of Responsible Tourism as designed in the UNWTO Global Code of Ethics. A number of issues required at a destination for the achievement of responsible tourism will be discussed eg civic behaviour, visitor ethics, environmental concepts and how this could be managed. Different types of tourism will be covered in this module include Niche Tourism and Alternative Tourism. Finally, the module will take the learner through the different stages of the Visitor Experience to achieve a better understanding of the difference between products and experience in Tourism.
At the end of the module/unit the learner will have been exposed to the following:

a)	Analyse critically the significance of responsible tourism as a vital tool in the effective implementation of sustainable and quality tourism;
b)	Define and critically appraise the advantages of adopting a sense of Civic Tourism (Shilling, 2007) for a better relationship between visitor and resident community;
c)	Describe and evaluate the negative outcomes from treating tourism as a product rather than experience;
d)	Discuss critically the importance of adding value in terms of experiences and diversity instead of basing these on price and availability only;
e)	Investigate the theoretical framework behind the concept of Responsible Tourism.
f)	Evaluate critically the value of Community-Based Tourism. At the end of the module/unit the learner will have acquired the responsibility and autonomy to:
a)	Carry out an assessment and discuss a destination, locality or region in terms of the aspects of responsibility and civic behaviour and their impact on the touristic activity taking place there;
b)	Deal with the community in managing tourism through a strong sense of civic responsibility;
c)	Quote the Global Code of Ethics (UNWTO) as a method of delivering responsible tourism;
d)	Distinguish between the ‘product’ and ‘experience’ within the concept of tourism. a)	Design a plan for promoting niche and alternative tourism to attract quality tourists;
b)	Create a strategy for establishing a locality or region as a quality  destination;
c)	Apply the theory of management of tourism to highlight the differences between tourism as a product and tourism as an experience;
d)	Evaluate the negative outcomes when tourism is treated as a product rather than tourism relating them to a context.

Module-Specific Learner Skills

At the end of the module/unit the learner will be able to

a.	Participate in face- to-face discussions during classes.
b.	Participate in teambuilding sessions  
c.	Communicate effectively

Hospitality and tourism businesses significantly contribute to the European and world economy in terms of job creation, GDP contribution and have significant impact on local communities. The contribution of tourism to Malta's GDP in 2018 was 12.8%, making tourism one of the top five contributors to the economy. Total tourist expenditure was EUR 2.1 billion, an increase of 8% from 2017. In 2018, there were 3.2 million international arrivals, an 11.0% increase on 2017. Whilst tourism, events and hospitality have been hit by the Covid pandemic, as the world emerges from the pandemic growth in these areas is set to reoccur.

The majority of tourism, events and hospitality businesses are small. However, the majority of research has been focused on management strategies of small business owners overlooking other perspectives. This module aims to provide students with a broader picture on tourism and hospitality entrepreneurs, encompassing both management and sociological perspectives and case studies from various settings, such as developing and transition economies. Such systematic and comprehensive overview of small businesses and entrepreneurship can add breadth and depth to knowledge and intellectual skill progression, and ultimate career development on graduation.
•	Be able to critically evaluate and apply, within the hospitality and tourism context, appropriate theories and concepts of entrepreneurship and enterprise development;
•	Be able to critically analyse and evaluate the business environment, its impact on the hospitality and tourism industry sectors and potential for entrepreneurship and enterprise development;
•	Critically apply the skills needed to effectively sell business products and/or services to discretionary purchasers;
•	Be able to critically assess the factors which can influence the development of enterprises operating within the hospitality and tourism sectors; 
•	Be able to critically interpret and synthesise understanding and knowledge relative to the vocational domain; and,
•	Demonstrate an advanced understanding of relevant ethical expectations and any professional regulatory compliance requirements as set out by national and international bodies. •	Understand entrepreneurs and entrepreneurialism in the modern world: definitions, trends and responses;
•	Understand Entrepreneurial behaviours and Social Enterprise activities; 
•	Research and assess theories, paradigms, principles and concepts of entrepreneurship and small business; and,
•	Analyse and understand factors which influence the development, growth and failure of hospitality and tourism businesses. •	Critically research and assess theories, paradigms, principles and concepts of entrepreneurship and small business;
•	Critically interpret the relationship of entrepreneurship and small businesses to society and the economy and demonstrate an understanding of the role of entrepreneurship and small businesses in developing, transition and developed economies;
•	Critically understand the factors which can influence the development, growth and failure of hospitality and tourism businesses and possible remedial strategies; and,
•	Critically understand the management of entrepreneurship and small business development in relation to Tourism, Events and Hospitality businesses.

This module will develop the student’s ability to manipulate data and use it efficiently. Quantitative data relates to statistical data including averages, quotas and percentages amongst others. This module is required to help students with their dissertation as well as in their future place of work. Furthermore, this module together with Qualitative Research Methods will prepare a student to prepare an effective dissertation research proposal.
a)	Demonstrate an understanding on the importance of the methodology section within a research project;
b)	Use different quantitative methods including questionnaires, polls and surveys to extract statistical data for a research initiative;
c)	Apply different approaches of quantitative research methods to the travel and hospitality industry context;
d)	Show an understanding of different tools to analyse quantititative data including basic knowledge of SPSS;
e)	Prepare a research proposal based on quantitative data;
f)	Create an analysis of quantitative data including the different approached to analysis like descriptive and inferential amongst others;
g)	Assemble data gathered through different methods of gathering quantitative statistical data in a way which is easy to decifher by the end-user; a)	Carry out semi-structured interviews, questionnaires, polls and/or surveys and extract relative quantitative data;
b)	Produce a research project by making proper use of quantitative data;
c)	Deal with and analyse different types of quantitative and numerical data;
d)	Guide others on using SPSS whilst also ensuring that the student can work with basic SPSS tools temselves;
e)	Supervise reasearch projects within the workplace; a)	Introduction to Research Methods;
b)	The Meaning of Methodology;
c)	Introduction to Quantitative Research Methods and what it entails 
d)	Data – Denifition and different types and forms of data;
e)	Quantitative Measurement and Sampling including Categorical Measurement, Nomical Measurement, Ordinal Measurement, Metric Measurement, Interval Measurement., Ratio Measurement and Discrete and Continous Measurement;
f)	Analysis of Quantitative Data including two data sets, concepts and variables;
g)	Descriptive Analysis – Nominal Categories, Ordinal Categories and Discrete and Grouped Data, Proportions, Percentages, Rations and Rates.
h)	Inferential Analysis – Hypothesis, Populations, Samples, Probability Theory, Sampling Methods, Sample Size and Response Rates;
i)	Answering Research Questions;
j)	Introduction to SPSS and what it entails;

Marketing increasingly occurs in a digital world and marketing graduates require specific knowledge and understanding of the impacts that digital technologies have had on marketing practice and consumer behaviour. The module introduces students to the principles of how digital marketing and social media can help organisations to gain a competitive advantage. Information and communication technology is constantly and rapidly changing the way companies interact with consumers. The module provides a framework for understanding the contextual, strategic and operational issues involved in the application of digital transformation to tourism, events and hospitality management.
•	the theoretical frameworks used to help address digital marketing challenges;
•	how alternative digital marketing strategies are designed to take advantage of changing consumer trends in tourism and events management; and,
•	how to locate and use appropriate marketing resources to research and produce marketing plans use digital technologies to address challenges. •	an ability to critically analyse the contemporary digital marketing landscape and the key challenges facing the industry;
•	an ability to critically understand core processes related to marketing in the digital world and the organisational context in which it is undertaken;
•	an ability to critically analyse the contribution of different digital technologies to effectively meet marketing goals;
•	an ability to critically understand and apply certain ethical and regulatory aspects of digital marketing communications and practices.
•	an ability to critically explore how companies make use of technological innovations to promote their activities; and,
•	an ability to critically develop and plan a digital campaign. •	Critically evaluate current developments, the opportunities and challenges of marketing technologies and discuss their future prospects;
•	Critically identify issues in developing marketing strategies for the web and the mobile wireless web;
•	Demonstrate a critical understanding and ability to analyse the issues involved in developing a digital marketing strategy in respect of tourism and events management;
•	Critically assess and design, implement and measure the success of an online promotional marketing campaign; 
•	Critically analyse digital marketing strategies for the web and the mobile wireless web; and,
•	Demonstrate an advanced understanding of relevant ethical expectations and any professional regulatory compliance requirements as set out by national and international bodies.

This module introduces Public Policy to International Hospitality Management students from a multi-level governance perspective: the international, national and local level of governance.
Students will be taught regarding (i) the role of international organisations (such as IMF, WTO, ILO), (ii) the EU and multinational organisations in Public Policy, (iii) the influence of national governments in policy-making and (iv) the contribution of the local government at a micro-level. Furthermore, students will be studying the core concepts of Public Policy that embrace the following: state, government, governance, society, separation of powers, pressure groups and civil society. A special emphasis will be made to the interconnection of tourism to public policy especially its contribution from an economic policy point-of-view and its influence vis-à-vis environmental, transport and social policy. An overview of the main public policy actors will also be delivered within the context of the policy-making process. Students will also be introduced to the public sector and public service domains.
a)	Introduction to Public Policy and what it entails;
b)	The Public Policy and the Public Policy making process vis-a-vis tourism policy;
c)	Inter-relationship between several policy areas and how tourism is interconnected to other policy domains;
d)	Democratic framework and how different interest groups could in reality influence tourism policy;
e)	Transition from government to governance, specifically the internal and external pressures to policy-making;
f)	Multi-level governance: The role of international organisations and the EU in policy-making, the main functions of national governments and local councils;
g)	Main policy players and how policy agendas are set;
h)	Legislative process;
i)	Difference between public sector and public service and the main components and functions of the public service; a)	Comply with public policies specifically to those related to tourism;
b)	Produce short reports and plans or be involved in a team working on a policy;
c)	Deal with a continuum of tourism stakeholders in a more pragmatic manner by having a better understanding of the governing structures and functioning of a state;
d)	Carry out tasks using relevant public policy jargon and key terms associated to both public and tourism policy; The learner will be able to:
a)	Demonstrate an understanding of public problems in tourism policies;
b)	Categorise public policy information in accordance to the respective domain;
c)	Apply the policy-process in various contexts and scenarios;
d)	Evaluate proposed public policy solutions;
e)	Compare and differentiate between local policies, national policies and white paper and a policy document;

This module covers the principles of sustainability both in terms of tourism and development through the Local Agenda 21 process. It also delves into tourism planning and development in terms of the long-term, continuous and consistent process of consultation as against short-term planning.
At the end of the module/unit the learner will have been exposed to the following: 
a)	Design a sustainable tourism plan for a destination, locality or region;
b)	Write a plan for tourism as a long-term socio-cultural activity;
c)	Apply the four stage method of planning through the integrated approach to any destination, locality and region.

Module-Specific Learner Skills

At the end of the module/unit the learner will be able to

a.	Participate in face- to-face or online discussions during classes.
b.	Participate in teambuilding sessions  
c.	Communicate effectively At the end of the module/unit the learner will have acquired the responsibility and autonomy to:
a)	Carry out an analysis of issues that may disrupt sustainability in tourism;
b)	Create a calendar of events and initiative aimed at high-end tourism;
c)	Deal with the key stakeholders in the tourism sector in an effective manner;
d)	Monitor initiatives and activities to ensure they are in line with the requirements of a quality destination a)	Develop an understanding and investigate the principles on which sustainable tourism is based;
b)	Define the principles of sustainable development and appraise critically  the impact it has on tourism;
c)	Describe and analyse critically the key principles on which sustainable tourism is based;
d)	Evaluate the basic processes of planning tourism;
e)	Define the stakeholders involved in tourism and analyze critically  the contribution of each in the process of planning in tourism;
f)	Critically evaluate the theoretical framework of sustainable tourism;
g)	Analyze critically  the key aspects of the history of sustainable tourism planning and show how an integrated approach to tourism planning can be applied referring to these aspects;
h)	Critically evaluate the four stage method of planning;

. The raison-d’etre behind this course is to enable participants to meet the challenge arising from running a leisure tourism enterprise such as a Scuba Diving Centre in a highly competitive environment yet adhering to rigorous health and safety standards.  It must be noted that around 6% of all the tourists visiting the Maltese islands come specifically to practice diving – recreational technical or free diving. In this respect, Malta can serve as an ideal case for the course participants to interact with.
-	Act  autonomously in carryingout risk assessment exercises, both at the place of work (dive centre, hyperbaric centre etc.) or at a particular dive site;

-	Systematically determine as to whether specific diving equipment has the right standards with respect to health and safety and approve it’s use or otherwise;


-	The ability to determine a person’s status following a potential exposure to a diving-related health hazard and indicate the treatment to be followed in order to stabilise the individual’s health status until medical help is available on site;

-	Business and marketing acumen in promoting diving related courses and other activities in order to improve the business profile of the diving enterprise;

-	ICT, social media and related tools used for research, further learning but also sales and marketing purposes. - Mentor and instruct learners (divers and non-divers) specific knowledge and skills (e.g. first aid courses, scuba diving/free diving courses) in order to ensure that they can safely practice this activity;
- Comprehensively understand how diving equipment works and demonstrate effectiveness and profecienct in servicing and repairing diving equipment;
- Systematically execute first aid and other medical procedures  (the latter under medical supervision)in order to stabilize a patient’s condition and/or  improve it. - Comprehensively understand the concepts of risk assessment with respect to scuba diving and creatively propose strategies how to reduce risks.
- Systematically understand the human physiology and acknowledge how this is affected by regular/frequent diving activity (recreational, technical and free diving);
- Comprehend the business elments, including the human resource management component and how does this operate in the leisure industry.
- Determine detailed knowledge on the various components making up diving equipment and specific equipment used for specific use (recreational, technical and free diving, research diving and other associated activities whilst diving)
- Systematically identify diving related hazards/injuries and their treatment both in terms of basic first aid but also more advanced procedures, such as the use of thevhyperbaric chamber;
-  Deploy established techniques on how to assist and partner with recompression chamber facilities, training and emergency assistance)

The travel and tourism industry comprises several main sectors including, by means of example, accommodation, visitor attractions, travel distribution, and sub-sectors of transportation including aviation, cruise, land-based transport and transport infrastructure. The module explores the economic and operational characteristics of each sector and sub-sector, and the ways in which they interact in the production of travel and tourism products and services.
•	Be able to critically examine systems, structures and the inter-relationships between the different sectors of the international tourism industry;
•	Be able to critically apply concepts relating to tourism demand and supply;
•	Be able to critically understand and apply the main issues relating to the management of international tourism destinations, including impact studies and sustainability;
•	Be able to critically examine business operations and management in the context of the international tourism industry; and,
•	Develop skills in critical thinking, group-working, communication, academic research and writing. •	Be able to critically describe the systems, structures and inter-relationships between different sectors of the international tourism industry;
•	Be able to demonstrate a critical understanding of how technology and innovation can enhance destinations and tourism experiences;
•	At a level consistent with MQF level 6 expectations, be able to outline concepts relating to international tourism demand and supply in the management of destinations and businesses;
•	Be able to  critically identify issues relating to impact studies and sustainability; 
•	Be able to  critically recognise management needs in the key business areas within the tourism sector; and,
•	Demonstrate an advanced understanding of relevant ethical expectations and any professional regulatory compliance requirements as set out by national and international bodies. •	critically identify the key components involved in the study and understanding of the travel and tourism industry, and appreciate the role of these sectors in travel and tourism as a whole;
•	critically understand the main economic and operating characteristics of accommodation, visitor attractions, travel distribution, aviation, cruise and land-based transport sectors;
•	critically identify the major operators and explain the industrial structure of the sectors; and,
•	critically evaluate the major influences on the sectors and explain the major trends.

Marketing plays an important role in the Tourism Industry. Tourism is the ‘World’s largest industry’. However, it is also a volatile industry and tourist organisations  operate in a very competitive market .  Modern organisations cannot operate without the use of marketing, so as to identify and satisfy their customers’ needs and communicate with them. 
In this unit, learners will explore marketing in the context of the travel and tourism sector, particularly when it comes to Diving Tourism, starting with a definition of marketing and an appreciation of the background and concepts of marketing. The learners will examine the factors that influence the marketing of services, studied in the context of consumer protection and the social responsibilities of organisations as well as economic and technological factors.
a.	Comprehensively understand the Six Main Categories of Market Research
b.	Deploy established techniques to to enquire on the five Marketing Concepts
c.	Determine detailed knowledge on the Marketing Mix in the Tourism Industry
d.	Systematically devise Marketing Plans
e.	Extend and apply knowledge on SWOT Analysis
f.	Systematically recognize the main principles of the BCG Matrix
g.	Comprehensively understand the core components of The Product Lifecycle a.	Show an understanding of the six main categories of market research and their uses;
b.	Demonstrate an understanding of the five marketing concepts and what they represent;
c.	Use the theory of marketing mix in the context of tourism services;
d.	Show how marketing for tourism is contributing to the success for this industry;
e.	Prepare a marketing plan for a tourist organisation;
f.	Construct a SWOT analysis for a tourist organisation;
g.	Design a promotional campaign for a tourist organisation;
h.	Show how to apply the BCG Matrix for a tourism product or service;
i.	Show an understanding of the tourism business environment, the individual motivations and buyer behaviour; a)	Autonomously carry out market research and analyse the reports generate from the research conducted;
b)	Systematically monitor the effectiveness of promotion activities in the tourism organisations;
c)	Creatively advise tourism organisations in designing a 7P framework so as to review and define the key issues that affect the marketing of their products and service;

Tourism is often seen as an industry based on economic gain and employment alone and the beneficiaries are just a select few rather than the key stakeholders including the local community, the local businesses and the local authorities. We need to understand that every member of the local community has a valid part to play in tourism as a socio-cultural activity through participation in the planning, development, implementation and management of this activity. To do this we need to understand who the tourist really is; what tourism is about and how we can play our part in creating a quality activity rather than a socio-economic industry based on numbers and quantity. This course will assist in bridging the gap between  theory, academia and practice; it is all about the application of these frameworks in the management of tourism – a term which is very different to Tourism Management which applies to those front-liners who deliver a sense of hospitality and service directly to the visitor as part of their career or profession in the airline, travel agency and hotel and catering sectors.  The objective of these modules and the entire course is to eliminate the fragmentation of tourism into separate and isolated sectors and work together for a sustainable, responsible and welcoming activity.

This course will offer learners the opportunity of building up their knowledge and skills in applying the theoretical aspects of the management of tourism in practice; it will help to “make things happen” rather than simply treat tourism as an industry that can result in short-term economic gain. The scope for this study unit and its modules will be to create a culture of professionalism in the delivery of hospitality and service; it will present the basis of a qualitative activity based on long-term planning and strategy building and it will use the integrated approach to tourism planning by using the principles of the Tourism Planning Triangle (Zarb, 2017) and Civic Tourism (Shilling, 2007). The study unit, through its modules, should create a drive for professionalism in the tourism activity and support students who want to specialize in one of the many sectors which this broad activity includes.
The learner will be able to: 
a) Apply the basic aspects of management to tourism with particular emphasis on communication; 
b) Show how the management of tourism could apply factors emerging from the development of the tourism industry in the past fifty years;
c) Apply the best principles and practices of consultation through the integrated approach to tourism planning;
d) Apply the four stage method of planning through the integrated approach to any destination, locality and region;
e) Plan tourism as a long-term socio cultural activity instead of a quantative short-term industry;
f) Design a sustainable tourism plan for a destination, locality or region;
g) Apply the Global Code of Ethics (UNWTO) as a method for delivering responsible tourism;
h) Analyse and compare the assets and issues on destinations that are based on a quantitative policy and others promoting qualitative aspects a) Develop a SWOT analysis of different touristic destinations 
b) Collaborate with other team members in delivering tourism-related projects
c) Design and manage a consultative campaign with stakeholders in the tourism sector in an effective way.
d) Analyse issues that may disrupt sustainability in tourism;
e) Create a calendar of events and initiative aimed at high-end tourism;
f) Appraise the key stakeholders in the tourism sector in an effective manner;
g) Evaluate critically a destination, locality or region in terms of the aspects of responsibility and civic behaviour and their impact on the touristic activity taking place there;
h) Collaborate with the community in managing tourism through a strong sense of civic responsibility;
i) Evaluate critically the Global Code of Ethics (UNWTO) as a method of delivering responsible tourism;
j) Analyse critically the difference between the ‘product’ and ‘experience’ within the concept of tourism.
k) Develop a Carrying Capacity Study for a destination and interpret results;
l) Create and develop a contingency plan of how a tourism activity can be managed in a time of change due unforeseen circumstances;
m) Reflect critically on the changes that are needed to maintain a competitive, sustainable, quality management of tourism process and enable new opportunities to be considered as an option. The learner will be able to: 
a)	Evaluate critically tourism as an activity based on quality;
b)	Analyse and compare the difference between destinations on the basis of quality;
c)	Analyse critically  the different management styles in tourism;
d)	Analyse and compare the different management styles in tourism over the course of the last 50 years of Maltese history.
e)	Critically appraise the current issues related to the tourism within a local and international context with particular emphasis how Covid 19 has affected this sector.
f)	Critically appraise the issues related to sustainability and responsibility in tourism
g)	Evaluate the need for long term planning in tourism
h)	Investigate the principles for sustainable and responsible tourism and how they  could be applied;
i)	Evaluate critically  how research and monitoring in tourism could be carried out;
j)	Analyse critically qualitative and quantitive data collated through research. 
k)	Critically evaluate various theories and practices widely known within the field of Tourism Management.

In today’s tourism industry, it is essential to have new professional figures who can satisfy the changing needs of consumers.  Tourists do not just want to visit a place, but they look for all that is related to the territory, starting with the food and its impact on society.  Hence the need for professionals, such as food and wine specialists having various skills and a good knowledge of the entire food production chain, starting from the food’s livestock and agricultural origins, right up to the industrial changes that it goes through.  The food and wine specialist is therefore in a position to associate and coordinate knowledge of the productive, regulatory, sanitary, hygienic, food safety, nutrition and economic aspects of the world of gastronomy, to its humanistic, artistic and aesthetic ones, even through the right communication skills.  
The graduate in gastronomic sciences can work in the agri-food chain, catering, food distribution and retail, preparation of typical foods and gastronomic products, protection and valorisation of local products, tourism and territorial promotion agencies, and public and private institutions of the sector.  But the possibilities are so many, considering that tourism plays such an important role in both Malta and Italy (the countries where VHEI hosts a campus), where food products are sought after by visitors.  In fact, tourists show much interest in the quality of local products and the cuisine of these Mediterranean countries.  The food and wine component, therefore, does not only reflect a steadily growing tourist segment, but it also constitutes a fundamental aspect of what attracts visitors to the two respective countries and contributes significantly to generate stable employment.
In line with VHEI’s educational philosophy, this programme of studies was designed to be practice-based and highly vocational, featuring both theoretical and practical activities – internships, workshops, masterclasses and cultural events.  For this reason, VHEI has designed its curriculum together with the Federazione Italiana Cuochi, with whom it will be working closely to deliver several of the study-units.  VHEI will also be collaborating with other partners operating in the industry, in both Malta and Italy, to ensure that the students are exposed to all the different facets of the world of gastronomy.
The Learner will be able to: 

- knowledge of professional and ethical responsibilities;
- knowledge of the business contexts and related economic, managerial and organizational aspects of the sector;
- knowledge of the techniques and language of tasting and gastronomic criticism;wledge of foodstuffs from the point of view of typical consumption contexts in the different geographical areas;
- knowledge of the history of gastronomy;
- knowledge of food through literature and art;
- knowledge of the methods and techniques of promoting food and wine products;
- adequate communication and marketing skills. The Learner will be able to: 

- Be familiar with the basics of biochemistry and food chemistry in order to satisfy the requirements of the relevant professions in gastronomy;
- Ability to use the proper vocabulary of chemistry.
- Apply the current national and international regulations, in terms of safety, transparency and traceability of food and wine products;
- Adapt and organise production and sales in relation to market demand, enhancing typical products;
- Adopt the research and analysis tools that may be useful in this subject area;
- Draw up legal and technical reports, as well as the necessary documentation to implement individual and corporate activities in the food and wine sector;
- Apply the principles of food legislation and legal sources, with particular attention to EU rules and standards. The Learner will be able to: 

- Skills and knowledge to promote food and wine tourism;
- Skills and knowledge to implement a territorial marketing action;

