This module acquaints learners with current principles and best practices in project management and applying this knowledge to event management and developing the learners’ ability to formulate, plan, manage and evaluate events. Teaching methods will include case studies.
•	Have a critically informed knowledge of project management principles and practices;
•	Have a critically informed knowledge of project integration management covering the elements of initiation, Planning, Execution, Monitoring and Controlling, and Closing;
Critically apply factual and theoretical knowledge in strategic management associated with the intended and emergent strategy development; 
•	Have a critically informed knowledge of project time and scope management integration management covering the elements of initiation, Planning, Execution, Monitoring and Controlling, and Closing; and,
•	Have a critically informed knowledge of project cost, resource and quality management.. •	Be able to  critically plan and manage a hospitality event project to a professional standard;
•	Be able to  critically evaluate events in terms of conceptual attractiveness, financial success, planning and operational delivery, pre and post-event variance analysis, sustainability, and ethics; and,
•	Be able to  critically identify aspects of their personal and professional development •	Be able to critically develop and execute an event management plan together with an industry standard risk assessment;
•	Be able to  critically discuss the role and responsibilities of the event manager, the project team and event stakeholders in successful event management;
•	Be able to  critically employ project management methodologies, tools and techniques to effectively plan an event; 
•	Be able to operate as part of an effective team to develop and critically synthesise the constituent elements of an event plan; and,
•	Demonstrate an advanced understanding of relevant ethical expectations and any professional regulatory compliance requirements as set out by national and international bodies

This module aims to provide the student with the fundamentals of economics in relation to the industry of hospitality and tourism.
a)	Monitor current economic trends;
b)	Manage the interpretation of economic trends using graphs, charts and tables;
c)	Be responsible for supporting the Tourism and Hospitality by producing and interpreting economics in a proficient manner;
d)	Carry out an economic analysis of a firm within the tourism industry;
e)	Deal with effects caused by economic issues; a)	Fundamental Economic Concepts; including the way the information generated could be interpreted and used the benefit of the tourism firm;
b)	Interpreting economic trends using graphs and related charts and tables;
c)	Evaluating economic trends, concepts and policies adhered within a specific environment;
d)	Understanding trends as produced by statistical reports from the National Statistical bodies and the Central Bank Report;
e)	The Relevance of Economics within Tourism and Hospitality Organisations;
f)	Supporting Tourism and Hospitality by producing and interpreting economic actions employed by the state or other players within the industry which could influence the operand of a smaller player or vice versa; The learner will be able to:
a)	Demonstrate an understanding of what economics is and the relationship between tourism and economics;
b)	Prepare a basic economic analysis of a firm within the tourism industry;
c)	Demonstrate whether one can interpret basic tourism related economic statistics;

